  "full_prompt": "Create an animation explaining the calculus behind backpropagation. Start by showing the feedforward process through a simple network (single neuron per layer) with labeled weights and biases. Define the cost function C0 = (a^L - y)^2 for a single training example. Show the activation formula a^L = σ(w^L·a^(L-1) + b^L). Introduce z^L = w^L·a^(L-1) + b^L as the weighted input before activation. Break the computation into a computational graph showing the chain of dependencies: weights and biases → z → a → cost. Use number lines to show the values of w, z, a, and C0, and visually demonstrate sensitivity by wiggling w and showing how it affects z, a, and C0. Show the chain rule derivatives: ∂C0/∂w^L = (∂C0/∂a^L)·(∂a^L/∂z^L)·(∂z^L/∂w^L). Compute each derivative explicitly: ∂C0/∂a^L = 2(a^L - y), ∂a^L/∂z^L = σ'(z^L), ∂z^L/∂w^L = a^(L-1). Show this gives ∂C0/∂w^L = 2(a^L - y)·σ'(z^L)·a^(L-1). Generalize to multiple neurons with indices j and k: ∂C0/∂w^L_jk = a^(L-1)_k · σ'(z^L_j) · ∂C0/∂a^L_j. Show the recursive nature: the error at layer L depends on the error at layer L+1 through the transpose of the weight matrix. Show the four fundamental backpropagation equations: δ^L = ∇_a C ⊙ σ'(z^L), δ^l = ((w^{l+1})^T δ^{l+1}) ⊙ σ'(z^l), ∂C/∂b^l_j = δ^l_j, ∂C/∂w^l_jk = a^{l-1}_k δ^l_j. Conclude with the full algorithm and how it computes the gradient efficiently.",
